    "body": "Fixes #1958. Fixes #2507.\n\nA term whose child already reports a mismatch against its expected type is no longer reported again itself, for tuple components and for function bodies. Tuple shape mismatches (arity, labels) say what differs.\n\n**Before.**\n- `let (a : Int, b : Int) = (1, \"s\") in a` produced three errors: `Expecting type Int but got inconsistent type String` on `\"s\"`, and `Expecting type Int, Int but got inconsistent type Int, String` on both the tuple and the parentheses around it. Arity and label mismatches were reported the same way, by printing both full types.\n- `let f : Int -> Bool = fun x -> x + 1 in f(1)` produced two errors: `Expecting type Bool but got inconsistent type Int` on `x + 1`, and `Expecting type Int -> Bool but got inconsistent type Int -> Int` on the whole `fun`, whose synthesized type was built from the already-marked body. With the function shorthand (`let f(x) = x + 1` as a module member checked against a signature) the second error sat on the synthetic `fun` the desugaring introduces, which has no source span.\n\n**Now.**\n- **Tuples.** A component that already reports a mismatch contributes its expected type to the tuple's synthesized type, so the tuple, and the parentheses that forward its marks, do not repeat it. A labeled element `l=e` does the same for its value. The first example is one error, on `\"s\"`.\n- **Functions.** A body that already reports a mismatch contributes the expected return type to the function's type, so the function is not marked again. The second example is one error, on `x + 1`. A non-function against an arrow type is still reported on itself.\n- **Tuple shape.** A mismatch of the tuple's own shape is still reported on the tuple and now says what differs: `Expecting a tuple of 2 elements but got 3 elements`, or `Expecting a tuple of 2 elements with labels name, age but got 1 element with labels name; missing label age`, in the CLI and the cursor inspector alike. `LabeledTupleStaticsHelpers.shape_mismatch` computes the summary once for both. A same-shape mismatch on a non-literal (a variable of tuple type) keeps the generic message, where the two types are the useful information.\n\nBoth rules are the one line `List.exists(Mark.is_expectation_mismatch, child.marks) ? expected : child.elab_syn_ty` at the parent's type construction, in the `Tuple`/`TupLabel` and `Fun` cases of `Statics.re`.\n\n**Tests.** The annotated-tree test for `(_=1) : (_=String)` pins the mark on the value only; tests for single reporting of a component and of a body (let-bound and as a function argument), for arity, and for a non-function against an arrow type; unit tests for `shape_mismatch`.\n\n## Verification\n\n`make dev` clean \u00b7 `make test-quick` green (3476 tests).\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
